) for a bounded orbit, the velocity would theoretically approach infinity (
$v \to ∞
) to conserve angular momentum. However, physical systems do not permit zero radii in this context. Conversely, as the radius approaches infinity (
$r \to ∞
) for a bound orbit, the velocity approaches zero (
$v \to 0
). For unbound (hyperbolic or parabolic) orbits, the velocity does not approach zero as the distance becomes very large; instead, the object escapes the central body's influence, and the product 
$v r
 generally increases or approaches a non-zero constant related to the asymptotic velocity.

